    "logic": {
      "concept": "A coastline reversal in intrinsic time. Clock time is replaced by events: a Directional Change is a reversal of at least 0.2% of price - scaled by how volatile the tape is now against its own recent median - and the Overshoot is the run that preceded it. The scaling law says an overshoot averages about one threshold, so an overshoot of 2.5x that is a stretched coastline, and the DC ending it is the entry. Volume at or above its mean and a volatility floor say the turn happened on participation rather than in an illiquid stall.",
      "entry": "LONG on an upward Directional Change that ends a downward overshoot of at least 2.5 thresholds, with volume >= SMA(20) x 1.0, ATR/close > 0.0005, and ADX(14) < 30 or close above EMA(200). SHORT mirrors it.",
      "exit": "The opposite Directional Change, or reversion to EMA(20), or the ATR stop (1.0x) or target (3.0x) - whichever the bar reaches first."
    }
  },
